#!/bin/sh
#
# Refuse ref changes while sub-agents are running.
#
# ## Why a hook and not an extension
#
# The obvious mechanism was a pi extension with a `tool_call` hook that blocks
# `git commit|tag|push`. It was written, installed, and tested three times, and
# it never fired:
#
#   * A project-local `.pi/extensions` entry is a resource that requires project
#     trust, and a sub-agent is spawned as `pi --mode json -p --no-session`. In
#     non-interactive mode the default `defaultProjectTrust: "ask"` ignores
#     project resources, so it was never loaded. The first sub-agent tested
#     against it duly cut a commit and a tag.
#   * A *global* extension has no trust requirement, but a diagnostic version
#     that logged every bash call wrote nothing when a sub-agent ran — so it is
#     not loading in those processes either, and a session cannot reload
#     extensions without a restart, so it could not be verified from inside the
#     session that installed it.
#
# Rather than ship a guard on faith, this uses git's own hook mechanism, which
# is verifiable in one command (see below) and does not depend on how a
# sub-agent process is launched.
#
# ## How it works
#
# `reference-transaction` fires for every ref update — commits, tags, resets,
# branch moves — so one hook covers all of them, including `git tag`, which has
# no dedicated hook. `pre-commit` is belt-and-braces for the commit case.
#
# Both refuse while `.git/AGENT-LOCK` exists. The main agent creates that file
# before spawning sub-agents and removes it afterwards, so the window during
# which nobody may cut a release is exactly the window in which agents are
# working.
#
# This is a guard against the *accidental* case, which is what actually
# happened: an agent finishing its work and helpfully committing and tagging it.
# It is not a sandbox — an agent that knew about the lock could remove it. It is
# deliberately not documented in any brief for that reason.
#
# ## Verifying
#
#   touch .git/AGENT-LOCK
#   git commit --allow-empty -m x     # must fail
#   git tag test-tag                  # must fail
#   rm .git/AGENT-LOCK
#   git commit --allow-empty -m x     # must succeed

if [ -f "$(git rev-parse --git-dir)/AGENT-LOCK" ]; then
  echo "refusing: sub-agents are running." >&2
  echo "Releases are cut by the main agent. Remove .git/AGENT-LOCK to override." >&2
  exit 1
fi

exit 0
